# Runtime Configuration

- Distinguish process configuration from persisted application settings. Environment variables configure startup, connectivity, credentials, transports, queues, and process topology.
- A `BaseSystemSetting` changes runtime behavior only when the consumer explicitly reads it from the settings store. Do not assume a similarly named UI setting overrides environment-backed process configuration.
- Hot authorization, audit, or settings *reads* on every request must not call definition-sync or other O(registered settings) walks. Sync definitions on boot and on write paths only. Request memoization must not leak mutable flags across Flask test-client requests that share one app context.
- Parse and validate environment values once in the owning runtime configuration layer. Pass typed configuration inward instead of repeatedly reading and coercing environment variables.
- Keep API, worker, and beat database, broker, result-backend, timezone, addon-path, and realtime settings aligned when they participate in the same application.
- Read secrets from environment or the deployment secret mechanism. Never place credentials, API keys, production secret defaults, or permissive origin policy in source, manifests, docs examples, or values files.
- Keep password and token settings write-only at API boundaries. Return presence/status metadata rather than stored secret values.
- Use stable setting keys. When renaming persisted settings, provide an explicit compatibility/migration path such as supported `legacy_keys`.
- Document precedence when a value may come from user settings, system settings, environment, or code defaults.
- State whether changing a setting is live, requires a worker/runtime restart, or requires redeployment. Do not present persisted-but-unconsumed settings as active configuration.
- Keep development defaults visibly development-only and fail safely when required production configuration is absent.
- Test the effective configuration source, parsing, precedence, secret redaction, and restart-sensitive behavior.
